            <ArticleTitle>Effects of angiotensin II and angiotensin II type 1 receptor blockade on neointimal formation after stent implantation.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>209-15</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">To evaluate the effect of supraphysiological levels of angiotensin II and selective angiotensin II type 1 receptor (AT1-receptor) blockade on neointimal formation and systemic endothelial function after stent implantation in the rat abdominal aorta.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Male Wistar rats were randomized to one of three groups; control (n=8), angiotensin II infusion (n=9, 200 ng/kg/min), or candesartan cilexetil (n=8,AT1-receptor blocker; rats received 14.4 mg kg(-1) day(-1)). Stents were implanted in the abdominal aorta. Histological analyses were performed at 4 weeks. Endothelial function was determined in isolated thoracic aortic rings.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Neointimal area was increased in the angiotensin II treated group versus the control group, 0.88 mm(2)+/-0.21 versus 0.66 mm(2)+/-0.16 (P&lt;0.05). Neointimal thickness was 171 microm+/-44 in angiotensin II treated animals and 120 microm+/-25 in the control group (P&lt;0.05). In addition, endothelial function was attenuated in angiotensin II treated animals (P=0.01). Candesartan cilexetil treatment did not result in reduction of neointimal area and did not reduce neointimal thickness compared to the control group. Candesartan had no effect on endothelial function.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Supraphysiological levels of angiotensin II aggravates neointimal formation in the stented rat abdominal aorta, and in parallel decreases endothelial function. AT1-receptor blockade does not reduce neointimal formation in rats without supraphysiological angiotensin II levels.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
